# Your first corpus

A corpus is a directory of documents that Loom reads together. This chapter takes an empty project to a corpus that writes a real file.

## Setting the project up

Run `loom init` in the project root.

```bash
loom init
```

It asks two questions. The first is the primary language, the language a section is written in when it does not say otherwise, and `typescript` is the default. The second is which languages should have editor services activated, which defaults to whatever you answered first.

Then it writes three things: `.loom/config.yaml`, holding those answers and the name of the directory Loom reads; `.loom/.gitignore`, so the services Loom downloads are not committed; and `.mcp.json`, which lets an agent talk to the workspace.

Init does not create the corpus directory — it only records its name. Make it yourself:

```bash
mkdir corpus
```

## Writing a chapter

A chapter is a Markdown file with a `.loom` extension. Write this into `corpus/01-greeting.loom`:

```loom
---
Part I: A greeting
Chapter 1: Saying hello
Target: src/
Language: TypeScript
---

# Saying hello

A greeting takes a name and returns a sentence. It is the smallest thing worth
writing down, and the smallest thing worth explaining.

## The greeting [greet.ts]

=>

export const greet = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`
```

The frontmatter says where the chapter sits and what it holds. `Target` names the directory its files are written into, and `Language` says what its code is written in.

The `#` heading opens a section. The `[greet.ts]` bracket on the second heading names the file that section's code belongs to, and `=>` opens the code itself.

## Tangling it

Point `loom tangle` at the corpus.

```bash
loom tangle corpus
```

It writes `src/greet.ts`, and what it writes is ordinary TypeScript — no marker, no import, nothing of Loom in it. A person reading that file cannot tell it came from a document.

Change the prose and tangle again and the file is untouched. Change the code and it is rewritten. The document is the source, and the file is what falls out of it.

## Reading it back

The same corpus renders as a document:

```bash
loom weave corpus/01-greeting.loom greeting.json
```

That gives you the chapter as data — its headings, its prose as Markdown, and its code — which is what a documentation site reads. These pages are exactly that, rendered from the corpus you are reading.
